Estimation moves points.
On $\R^\dimension$ a step is an addition, $\point + \tangent$.
On a curved manifold, such as the rotations or the unit sphere, there is no addition, and a *retraction* takes its place.
A retraction is defined by its derivative, so this note first recalls what a derivative on a manifold is.
It then fixes the coordinates in which tangent vectors are written, defines a retraction, shows how it turns every tangent space into a chart, and ends with the notation that the later notes use.
After this note, a tangent vector is a column of numbers and a derivative is a matrix.

## Smooth manifolds and smooth maps

The theory of smooth manifolds is set out in [Chapters 1 and 2](cite:lee2013).
This note recalls only what it uses, and proves none of it.

A smooth manifold $\manifold$ of dimension $\dimension$ is a topological manifold with a smooth structure: a maximal atlas of charts $(U, \varphi)$, each a homeomorphism from an open $U \subseteq \manifold$ onto an open subset of $\R^\dimension$, whose transition maps are smooth.
Let $\otherManifold$ be a second smooth manifold, and write $\dimx$ and $\dimy$ for the dimensions of $\manifold$ and $\otherManifold$.
A map $\map : \manifold \to \otherManifold$ is of class $C^k$ when it is continuous and, for every chart $(U, \varphi)$ of $\manifold$ and every chart $(V, \psi)$ of $\otherManifold$, its coordinate representation

$$
	\psi \circ \map \circ \varphi^{-1} : \varphi\bigl(U \cap \map^{-1}(V)\bigr) \to \R^\dimy
$$

is of class $C^k$.
Continuity makes $U \cap \map^{-1}(V)$ open, so the representation is a map between open subsets of Euclidean spaces.
Because the transition maps are smooth, it is enough to check this for the charts of any one atlas.
These maps form $C^k(\manifold, \otherManifold)$, and a *smooth* map is one of class $C^\infty$.
A *diffeomorphism* is a smooth bijection whose inverse is smooth, and a map from an open subset of $\manifold$ onto an open subset of $\R^\dimension$ is a chart of the smooth structure exactly when it is a diffeomorphism.

## Tangent vectors and the tangent bundle

Tangent vectors are set out in [Chapter 3](cite:lee2013).
This note uses four facts from it.

1. Each point $\point$ has a *tangent space* $\T_\point\manifold$, a real vector space of dimension $\dimension$.
   A smooth map $\map$ has at each point a *differential* $\dd\map_\point : \T_\point\manifold \to \T_{\map(\point)}\otherManifold$, a linear map, and the differentials obey the chain rule, $\dd(G \circ \map)_\point = \dd G_{\map(\point)} \circ \dd\map_\point$.
2. A chart $(U, \varphi)$ around $\point$ gives a basis of $\T_\point\manifold$, its *coordinate basis*.
   In the coordinate bases of charts around $\point$ and $\map(\point)$, the matrix of $\dd\map_\point$ is the Jacobian of the coordinate representation at $\varphi(\point)$.
3. A finite-dimensional vector space $E$ has each tangent space $\T_e E$ identified with $E$ itself, canonically.
   On $\R^n$ this makes the differential of a map the Jacobian.
4. The *tangent bundle* $\T\manifold$, the disjoint union of the tangent spaces, is a smooth manifold of dimension $2\dimension$.
   Its charts are built from those of $\manifold$: a point read in a chart, paired with the components of a tangent vector in that chart's coordinate basis.
   It contains the *zero section*, the zero vectors $0_\point \in \T_\point\manifold$.

## Tangent coordinates

A computation does not handle derivations.
It writes a tangent vector as a column of $\dimension$ numbers, so every tangent space needs a basis.
Fix, for each point $\point$, a linear isomorphism

$$
	\basis_\point : \R^\dimension \to \T_\point\manifold , @tag(basis)
$$

whose columns $\basis_\point e_1, \dots, \basis_\point e_\dimension$ are a basis of the tangent space at $\point$.
The coordinate basis of a chart around $\point$ is one choice, and every choice is the coordinate basis of some chart.
On $\R^n$, and on any vector space, the convention is the canonical identification of fact 3.

No regularity in $\point$ is asked of the convention, and it need not be defined everywhere.
It may be given on an open subset of $\manifold$ only, and everything below is then said of points of that subset, with every computation kept inside it.
The unit sphere $S^2 \subset \R^3$ is an example: by the hairy ball theorem it has no continuous basis of its tangent planes, so a convention that is smooth must leave out a point, such as the south pole.
On a Lie group, the left translations of one basis at the identity give a convention that is defined and smooth everywhere.

In these coordinates the differential of a smooth map is a matrix, the *derivative*

$$
	\D\map(\point) \defeq \basis_{\map(\point)}^{-1} \circ \dd\map_\point \circ \basis_\point \in \R^{\dimy \times \dimx} . @tag(derivative)
$$

On $\R^n$ it is the Jacobian.
The chain rule of fact 1 becomes a product of matrices, since the bases at $\map(\point)$ cancel.

<Statement {...chainRule} />

A convention without regularity serves everything that happens at one point: the derivative, the chain rule and, below, the tangent chart at $\point$.
It does not serve what compares the coordinates at nearby points, such as differentiating a function of $\point$ that is read in the coordinates at $\point$.
The convention is *smooth near* $\point$ when, on an open $W \ni \point$, the map $(\otherPoint, \tangent) \mapsto \basis_\otherPoint \tangent$ is smooth from $W \times \R^\dimension$ to $\T\manifold$.
Equivalently, its columns form a smooth local frame on $W$ [Chapter 8](cite:lee2013).
Results that compare nearby points assume this, and [%full](statement:smoothness) restates it in terms of the retraction.

## Retractions

A retraction is defined on the tangent bundle, without reference to any basis, as it is in optimization on manifolds [Section 4.1](cite:absil2008).

<Statement {...retraction} />

The retraction at $\point$ is the restriction $\retr_\point$ of $\retr$ to $\T_\point\manifold$.
Its condition on the differential is coordinate-free, so it holds whatever the convention.
Read in the coordinates [](eq:basis), the retraction at $\point$ is a map from $\R^\dimension$, written as an addition:

$$
	R_\point \defeq \retr_\point \circ \basis_\point : \R^\dimension \to \manifold ,
	\qquad \point \oplus \tangent \defeq R_\point(\tangent) . @tag(oplus)
$$

The two conditions of the definition become, for every convention,

$$
	\point \oplus 0 = \point
	\qquad \text{and} \qquad
	\D R_\point(0) = \basis_\point^{-1} \circ \dd(\retr_\point)_{0_\point} \circ \basis_\point = I_\dimension , @tag(conditions)
$$

where $\R^\dimension$ carries its canonical convention.
The derivative of a retraction at the origin is the identity, in the sense of [](eq:derivative).

<Statement {...smoothness} />

<Proof>

If the convention is smooth on $W$, then $(\point, \tangent) \mapsto \point \oplus \tangent$ is the composite of the smooth map $(\point, \tangent) \mapsto \basis_\point \tangent$ with $\retr$.
If instead $\oplus$ is smooth on $W \times \R^\dimension$, then by [](eq:conditions) $\basis_\point \tangent = \dd(\retr_\point)_{0_\point}(\basis_\point \tangent)$ is the velocity at $t = 0$ of the curve $t \mapsto \point \oplus t\tangent$.
That velocity is the differential of $\oplus$ at $(\point, 0)$ applied to $(0, \tangent)$, and so depends smoothly on $(\point, \tangent)$.

</Proof>

The definition is the one used in optimization on manifolds, where retractions replace the exponential map of a Riemannian manifold.
The exponential map is a retraction, but so is any map that agrees with it to first order, which is usually cheaper.
Nothing here needs a metric.

Two examples show the retraction and the convention apart.

1. On $\R^\dimension$, with the canonical convention, the retraction is $\retr_\point(w) = \point + w$, so $\point \oplus \tangent = \point + \tangent$.
   This is the addition that retractions replace on curved manifolds.
2. On the positive reals $\positive = (0, \infty)$, each $\T_\point\positive$ is $\R$ canonically, and $\retr_\point(w) = \point\, e^{w / \point}$ is a retraction: it is defined for every $w$, never leaves the positive reals, and has derivative $1$ at $w = 0$.
   With the convention $\basis_\point(\tangent) = \point\, \tangent$, which measures a tangent vector as a relative change, $\point \oplus \tangent = \point\, e^{\tangent}$.

## Tangent charts

By [](eq:conditions), the differential of $R_\point$ at $0$ is $\basis_\point$, an isomorphism.
The inverse function theorem for manifolds [Chapter 4](cite:lee2013) makes $R_\point$ a diffeomorphism from an open neighbourhood of $0 \in \R^\dimension$ onto an open neighbourhood of $\point$.
Its inverse is a chart of $\manifold$ centred at $\point$, the *tangent chart* at $\point$, and every point near $\point$ has coordinates in it.
We write

$$
	\otherPoint \ominus \point \defeq R_\point^{-1}(\otherPoint) @tag(ominus)
$$

for $\otherPoint$ in the neighbourhood where $R_\point$ is invertible.
In words, $\otherPoint \ominus \point$ is where $\otherPoint$ sits in the chart at $\point$.
By construction,

$$
	(\point \oplus \tangent) \ominus \point = \tangent @tag(roundtrip)
$$

for every $\tangent$ in the chart's domain, and $\point \oplus (\otherPoint \ominus \point) = \otherPoint$ for every $\otherPoint$ in its image.
On $\R^\dimension$, $\oplus$ and $\ominus$ are addition and subtraction; on the positive reals above, $\otherPoint \ominus \point = \log(\otherPoint / \point)$.

The coordinate basis of the tangent chart at its centre is $\basis_\point$, again by [](eq:conditions).
So the tangent chart reproduces the convention at $\point$, though at other points its coordinate basis need not be the convention's.

## Derivatives through the retraction

Since $\D R_\point(0) = I_\dimension$, its inverse has $\D(\cdot \ominus \point)(\point) = I_\dimension$ too, and the chain rule turns the derivative [](eq:derivative) into an ordinary Jacobian.
This is how every later note computes a derivative.

<Statement {...throughRetraction} />

<Proof>

Near $0$, $(\cdot \ominus \otherPoint) \circ \map \circ (\point \oplus \cdot)$ is a composite of three smooth maps, and [%full](statement:chain-rule) gives its derivative at $0$ as $\D(\cdot \ominus \otherPoint)(\otherPoint)\, \D\map(\point)\, \D R_\point(0) = \D\map(\point)$.
Its domain and codomain are open subsets of Euclidean spaces with their canonical conventions, so this derivative is the Jacobian.

</Proof>

The *local representative* of $\map$ at $\point$ is $\hat\map_\point = (\cdot \ominus \otherPoint) \circ \map \circ (\point \oplus \cdot)$, and [%full](statement:through-retraction) says that $\D\map(\point)$ is its Jacobian at $0$.
Applied to a direction, it is a derivative along a curve,

$$
	\D\map(\point)\, \tangent = \frac{\dd}{\dd t}\Big|_{t=0} \bigl(\map(\point \oplus t\tangent) \ominus \otherPoint\bigr) , @tag(directional)
$$

which is also how it is checked by finite differences.
To derive one by hand:

1. perturb the input, $\point_t = \point \oplus t \tangent$;
2. push it through, $\otherPoint_t = \map(\point_t)$;
3. solve $\otherPoint_t = \otherPoint \oplus \otherTangent(t)$ for $\otherTangent(t)$;
4. the Jacobian–vector product is $\otherTangent'(0)$, and the vector–Jacobian product is its transpose.

## The mirror identity

On $\R^\dimension$, $\point \ominus (\point \oplus \tangent) = -\tangent$ as well: the step back from $\point \oplus \tangent$ to $\point$ is the reverse of the step forward.
On a curved manifold it generally is not, because the left side is measured in the chart at the *moved* point $\point \oplus \tangent$, not at $\point$.
So it also compares the coordinates at two points, and holds even to first order only when the convention is smooth.

<Statement {...mirror} />

<Proof>

Write $\varphi(\tangent, c) = R_\point^{-1}(R_{\point \oplus \tangent}(c))$, the chart at $\point \oplus \tangent$ read in the chart at $\point$.
It is smooth near $(0, 0)$, because $\oplus$ is smooth near $\point$, with $\varphi(\tangent, 0) = \tangent$ and, since $R_{\point \oplus 0} = R_\point$, $\varphi(0, c) = c$.
Hence $\varphi(\tangent, c) = \tangent + c + O(\lvert\tangent\rvert\,\lvert c\rvert)$.
The coordinate $c = \point \ominus (\point \oplus \tangent)$ is the one with $\varphi(\tangent, c) = R_\point^{-1}(\point) = 0$, so $c = -\tangent + O(\lvert\tangent\rvert\,\lvert c\rvert)$, and $\lvert c \rvert = O(\lvert\tangent\rvert)$ gives the claim.

</Proof>

The identity holds exactly in some cases: on $\R^\dimension$, on the positive reals above, and on any Lie group whose retraction is $R_\point(\tangent) = \point \operatorname{Exp}(\tangent)$, since then $\point \ominus (\point \oplus \tangent) = \operatorname{Log}(\operatorname{Exp}(\tangent)^{-1}) = -\tangent$.
It fails at second order for other retractions, and a computation that needs "the point whose coordinate is $-\tangent$" must not assume it.

A manifold whose retraction is $\point + \tangent$, so that both identities hold exactly and every chart is the same affine chart, is called *flat* in these notes.

## Notation from here on

The later notes never write $\retr$, $\dd\map_\point$ or $\basis_\point$.
They write a tangent vector as a column of numbers attached to a point, and keep the name $\T_\point\manifold$ for the space of those columns, so that it stays visible which point a vector belongs to.
Inside this note $\T_\point\manifold$ is the space of fact 1; after it, it is its coordinate space $\R^\dimension$.
Every such abuse of notation stands for an object of this note read through the convention [](eq:basis), and this table lists them all.

| written later as | stands for | first used in |
| --- | --- | --- |
| $\T_\point\manifold = \R^\dimension$, the tangent space at $\point$ | $\T_\point\manifold$, read through $\basis_\point$ | every later note |
| $\tangent \in \T_\point\manifold$, a column of $\dimension$ numbers | the tangent vector $\basis_\point \tangent$ | every later note |
| a frame, a $\dimension \times n$ matrix of tangent vectors at $\point$ | $n$ tangent vectors, each read through $\basis_\point$ | automatic differentiation; gradients and Hessians |
| a cotangent $\alpha \in \R^\dimension$, a column acting by $\alpha\transpose \tangent$ | the covector $\alpha\transpose \basis_\point^{-1}$ on $\T_\point\manifold$ | gradients and Hessians |
| $\D\map(\point) \in \R^{\dimy \times \dimx}$ | the differential $\dd\map_\point$, by [](eq:derivative) | this note, and every later one |
| $\D\map(\point)\transpose$, applied to a cotangent | the pull-back of a covector by $\dd\map_\point$ | gradients and Hessians |
| the gradient $g(\point) = \D f(\point)\transpose 1$ | the differential $\dd f_\point$, not a Riemannian gradient | gradients and Hessians |
| $\point \oplus \tangent$, $\otherPoint \ominus \point$, $R_\point$, $R_\point^{-1}$ | $\retr(\basis_\point \tangent)$ and its local inverse | this note, and every later one |
| pairs $(\point, \tangent)$ with $\tangent \in \R^\dimension$ | the tangent bundle $\T\manifold$ over the convention's domain | this note |
| "$\oplus$ is smooth near $\point$" | the convention is a smooth local frame near $\point$ | this note; gradients and Hessians; densities in tangent charts |
| $\T_\point\manifold = \T_{\point_1}\manifold_1 \oplus \cdots \oplus \T_{\point_k}\manifold_k$, stacked columns | the direct sum, with the convention that is each factor's on its block | product manifolds |
| Lebesgue measure on $\T_\point\manifold$, and densities with respect to it | Lebesgue measure on $\R^\dimension$, carried to $\T_\point\manifold$ by $\basis_\point$ | densities in tangent charts; the normal |

The last row is where the convention matters most.
A density on a tangent space changes by the absolute determinant of a change of basis, so a density "on $\T_\point\manifold$" is always one in the coordinates the convention gives.
